<div class="mw-parser-output"><h1>Flail</h1>
<p>Average Length: shaft approx. 3 to 4 feet; chain and ball approx. 1 to 1.5 feet.
Type of Weapon: Close Hand-to-Hand.
Commonly used by: Flail-men.
</p><p>Also called a 'ball and chain', this was somewhat similar to a mace. The spiked ball, however, was mounted on a chain or strap which was attached to a stick. Much momentum could be generated by swinging it, and if you could control it it was extremely dangerous. The flail was very similar to the mace in theory, that is, put a hard object with mass on the end of a stick so you could build up momentum before striking the target. In flails, separating the striking object (example: metal balls, spiked or not) from the haft by a chain gave flails flexibility. This flexibility increased the impact force of the strike and made parrying the attack much more difficult. While also similar in concept to a morning star or ball and chain, the flail generally consisted of multiple chains and balls attached to a haft, thus, the flail was even more of a weapon impossible to parry against. The earliest flails were merely adaptations of agricultural tools (grain thresher, for example) but served well as weapons of war.
</p>
<h2><span class="mw-headline" id="In_Valhalla">In Valhalla</span></h2>
<p>In Valhalla the flail is a weapon skill of its own, one of the clubs and maces (see <a cmd='#'>Help Weapons</a>): two-handed, it bludgeons, it leans on strength, with some dexterity, and it is slow, as two-handed weapons are. It is taught by the barbarians from guild level 14, the fighters from guild level 14, the necromancers from guild level 14 (up to 70%), the knights from guild level 15, the paladins from guild level 15 and the healers from guild level 34 (up to 70%).
</p><p><br />
